  arguments to all those points but at  least they were making the argument  right there they were saying we need  this amount of force because of this  threat we're facing is significant and  we need to protect you know officer  safety once California legalize medical  marijuana and the federal government now  starts sending SWAT teams and to raid  these dispensaries that justification  goes out the window right I mean these  are businesses that are openly operating  they have business licenses they're  operating in compliance with state law I  mean nobody's can seriously argue that  the you know hippie mom-and-pop couple  who run the pot dispensary our threat to  you know pull an ar-15 out from under  the counter and kill a bunch of federal  agents this was about sending a  political message these these people  were openly flouting federal law and so  we had to bring the boot down on them to  make an example of them and this is  really sort of terrifying territory that  we enter in the 1990s and of course it's  still going on today this is government  using violence to send a political  message and that's you know that's not  something we normally associate with  free societies the next sort of frontier  I think comes there wasn't sort of a  make-or-break moment when it happened  but I think in just in the last probably  seven or eight years we've really seen  SWAT mission creep grow beyond the drug  war and now you know as you said we're  seeing these raids on poker games  particular when the Texas Hold'em craze  started taking off in the mid 2000s we  saw SWAT teams breaking up poker  neighborhood poker games poker games at  bars that were openly advertised were  raided with SWAT teams you know reason  TV did the story about the the VFW hall  in Dallas that was raided by a SWAT team  because they were conducting charity  poker games and here's where we really  start to see the mission creep and SWAT  sort of looking for new reasons to use  the sort of force you 